        title: 'Sodium Channel Blockade — Diagnostic Criteria',
        body: '**What you are looking for:** a wide complex tachycardia caused by a drug blocking the cardiac fast sodium channel, rather than by a ventricular focus. The distinction matters because it flips the treatment to its opposite.\n\n---\n\n### The drugs that do this\n\nThe American Heart Association 2023 poisoning update names these sodium channel blockers:\n\n• **Tricyclic and tetracyclic antidepressants (TCAs)** — amitriptyline, amoxapine, clomipramine, desipramine, doxepin, imipramine, maprotiline, nortriptyline, protriptyline, trimipramine\n• **Antiarrhythmics** — flecainide, propafenone, quinidine\n• **Anticonvulsants** — carbamazepine, lamotrigine, lacosamide, topiramate, zonisamide\n• **Antimalarials** — chloroquine, hydroxychloroquine, quinine\n• **Others** — cocaine, diphenhydramine, venlafaxine, thioridazine, yew (*Taxus* species)\n\nAlso consider **local anesthetics** in overdose (bupivacaine, ropivacaine) — these have a distinct antidote, covered later in this pathway.\n\n---\n\n### ECG criteria, and how good each one actually is\n\n**Terminal R wave in lead aVR ≥3 mm** — the final upward deflection in aVR, three small boxes tall or more.\nIn tricyclic overdose: **81% sensitive, 73% specific.** At ≥5 mm it becomes 97% specific but only 50% sensitive. This was the only variable to survive multivariable regression in the derivation study.\n\n**R/S ratio in aVR >0.7** — R wave height divided by S wave depth in aVR.\n**75% sensitive.** Odds ratio 16 for serious arrhythmia across psychotropic overdose (14.5 in the tricyclic-only subset). A ratio at or below 0.7 carries a negative predictive value around **95%** — the best rule-out available here — but that still leaves roughly 1 in 20, and the positive predictive value is only 41%.\n\n**QRS width** — the classic teaching is that QRS ≥100 ms predicts seizures and ≥160 ms predicts ventricular arrhythmia.\n⚠️ **Treat those thresholds with real suspicion.** In a case-control comparison, QRS ≥100 ms appeared in 82% of psychotropic-overdose patients who went on to a serious arrhythmia — but also in **76% of those who did not**. QRS ≥160 ms was only **13% sensitive**. The authors concluded that using either cutoff to predict arrhythmia "is not supported by our study." A pooled meta-analysis of 18 studies reached the same verdict: the ECG and the serum drug level both perform poorly.\n\n**The number that actually matters is the change from this patient\'s own baseline.** A QRS that has widened from a previously narrow tracing is acquired blockade, not a pre-existing bundle branch block.\n\n**Wide out of proportion to the rate** — QRS ≥160 ms at a rate under 130 bpm. Not a formally validated criterion, but a well-described pattern: poisoned conduction is wide *and* slow, whereas ventricular tachycardia wide enough to look like this is usually fast.\n\n**Flecainide specifically:** in a systematic review of 22 published toxic ECGs, deaths occurred only in patients with QRS >200 ms. Flecainide toxicity readily masquerades as ventricular tachycardia, with a bizarre bundle branch block pattern and sometimes a northwest axis. The tip-off is a heart rate out of proportion to how unwell the patient looks. (Published cases only — this is not an incidence estimate.)\n\n---\n\n### 🛑 Why the standard wide complex algorithms will not rescue you here\n\nThe Brugada and Vereckei algorithms were derived in patients whose rhythm diagnosis was proven by electrophysiology study — a population that structurally excludes poisoned patients. **Neither has ever been tested in overdose.** Even within their own population, a head-to-head comparison of five published methods found positive likelihood ratios of only **1.56 to 3.46**. Do not let an "SVT" answer from these algorithms talk you out of a poisoning.\n\n→ Full toxidrome protocol: [TCA Toxidrome](#/tree/tca-toxidrome)',
