        title: 'Sodium Channel Blockade — Treatment',
        body: '🛑 **DO NOT GIVE PROCAINAMIDE.** It is a Vaughan-Williams class Ia sodium channel blocker. Giving it here adds a second blockade to a conduction system already poisoned by the first.\n\n🛑 **DO NOT GIVE AMIODARONE.** It carries sodium channel blocking activity alongside its other effects.\n\n---\n\n### Sodium bicarbonate — the treatment\n\n**Bolus:** [Sodium Bicarbonate](#/drug/sodium-bicarbonate/tca toxicity) **50–150 mEq intravenous push** in an adult; **1–3 mEq/kg** in a child.\n**Repeat every 3–5 minutes** while the QRS remains wide or the patient remains unstable.\n\n**Infusion:** 150 mEq of sodium bicarbonate in 1 litre of D5W, run at **1–3 mL/kg/hour**. Add 20–40 mEq of potassium chloride per litre — alkalinization drives potassium into cells, so the hypokalemia is expected, not incidental.\n\n**How it works — both halves of the molecule matter.** Alkalinization reduces drug binding at the sodium channel, and the sodium load raises the electrochemical gradient across it. Laboratory work in cardiac Purkinje fibres shows the balance varies by drug: for imipramine the effect was attributable entirely to alkalinization, for flecainide and mexiletine both alkalinization and the sodium load contributed, and for disopyramide bicarbonate did not help at all. Note that while the *toxicity* of tricyclics is rate-dependent, the *reversal* by bicarbonate is not — it works equally well at any heart rate.\n\n### 🛑 Hard stops — these are the endpoints, not the QRS\n\n• **Arterial pH 7.55**\n• **Serum sodium 155 mEq/L**\n\nStop escalating at those numbers **even if the QRS is still wide**. Some poisoned QRS complexes never fully narrow, and chasing a normal-looking QRS past these limits is how patients get hurt. Case reports describe profound alkalemia (pH >7.60) when bicarbonate boluses are stacked on top of hyperventilation in an intubated patient; severe alkalemia carries higher mortality in other settings.\n\n**While on bicarbonate, recheck every 30–60 minutes:** blood gas, sodium, potassium, chloride. Watch specifically for hypernatremia, alkalemia, hypokalemia, and hypochloremia.\n\n---\n\n### Supportive care that changes the outcome\n\n• **Ventilation.** Acidemia worsens drug binding at the channel, so do not allow the patient to hypoventilate. But do not deliberately hyperventilate on top of bicarbonate boluses either — that combination is what produces pH >7.60. Confirm with a blood gas, not with the ventilator settings alone.\n• **Seizures — benzodiazepines.** Every seizure generates an acidosis that deepens the cardiotoxicity. Avoid phenytoin: it is itself a sodium channel blocker.\n• **Hypotension** — isotonic crystalloid first, then norepinephrine.\n• **Call your poison center early.** United States: **1-800-222-1222.**\n\n---\n\n**Evidence grade (American Heart Association 2023):**\n• **Class 1, Level B-NR** — sodium bicarbonate for life-threatening cardiotoxicity from tricyclic or tetracyclic antidepressant poisoning.\n• **Class 2a, Level C-LD** — sodium bicarbonate for life-threatening cardiotoxicity from any other sodium channel blocker.\n• **Class 2a, Level C-LD** — sodium bicarbonate for wide complex tachycardia or cardiac arrest from cocaine poisoning.\n\n→ Full protocol: [TCA Toxidrome](#/tree/tca-toxidrome)',
