        body: '**First, confirm this is genuinely refractory.** Have you actually given adequate bicarbonate, and have the pH and sodium not yet reached the hard stops (pH 7.55, sodium 155 mEq/L)? Refractory means refractory to *adequate* therapy.\n\n---\n\n### Lidocaine — yes, genuinely, and this is a change from older teaching\n\nThis is counterintuitive, so read it carefully. Lidocaine is a Vaughan-Williams **class Ib** sodium channel blocker. Unlike the class Ia and class Ic agents, it binds and releases the channel very rapidly, so it can displace the offending drug without producing sustained blockade of its own.\n\n• **Class 2a, Level C-LD** — lidocaine for wide complex tachycardia from **cocaine** poisoning.\n• **Class 2b, Level C-LD** — lidocaine for life-threatening cardiotoxicity from **class Ia or class Ic** sodium channel blockers (quinidine, procainamide, disopyramide, flecainide, propafenone).\n\n⚠️ This is a specific, graded exception to the general rule that antiarrhythmics are contraindicated here. It is **not** permission to give amiodarone or procainamide. The evidence is weak (Level C-LD). Discuss with your poison center or a medical toxicologist first.\n\n---\n\n### Intravenous lipid emulsion — the class depends entirely on the drug\n\nThe same intervention carries **three different recommendation classes** in the same guideline. Getting this wrong in either direction causes harm:\n\n• **Class 1, Level C-LD** — for **local anesthetic** poisoning (bupivacaine, ropivacaine). Here it is a genuine antidote: 20% lipid emulsion 1.5 mL/kg bolus, then 0.25 mL/kg/min infusion.\n• **Class 2b, Level C-LD** — for other sodium channel blocker poisoning, and **only when refractory to everything else**. Last line. A randomized trial found no benefit in tricyclic poisoning, and lipid may actually increase absorption of an oral overdose still sitting in the gut.\n• **Class 3 (No Benefit), Level C-LD** — for beta-blocker and calcium channel blocker poisoning.\n\n---\n\n### Extracorporeal support — and the trap next to it\n\n• **VA-ECMO (venoarterial extracorporeal membrane oxygenation) — Class 2a, Level C-LD** for refractory cardiogenic shock, refractory dysrhythmia, or cardiac arrest from sodium channel blocker poisoning. **Call early.** The poison wears off and the myocardium recovers, so this is a bridge with a defined endpoint — one of the better indications for ECMO in all of emergency medicine.\n\n• 🛑 **Do NOT confuse this with dialysis.** The EXTRIP workgroup recommends **against** extracorporeal drug removal (hemodialysis, hemoperfusion) in tricyclic poisoning — **Grade 1D**, a strong recommendation. Tricyclics are highly protein-bound with a large volume of distribution and are simply not dialyzable. Dialysis will not clear the drug; ECMO supports the circulation until the patient clears it themselves. These two point in opposite directions and are easy to conflate.\n\n---\n\n### Other measures\n\n• **Hypertonic saline (3% sodium chloride)** — if the pH ceiling has been reached but more sodium is still wanted, this delivers sodium without further alkalinization. Low-quality evidence; toxicologist-directed.\n• **Magnesium** if there is any QT prolongation or polymorphic component.\n• **Cardioversion or defibrillation is safe in both diagnoses.** If the patient is unstable, shock — do not delay electricity while debating the drug.\n\n**Escalate in parallel, not in sequence:** medical toxicology / poison center, cardiology, and the ECMO team all at once.',
