        title: 'Three Mimics of Drug-Induced Sodium Channel Blockade',
        body: 'A wide, slow rhythm has three common non-toxicologic causes that look nearly identical on the tracing. Send the tests — the ECG cannot reliably sort them.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Hyperkalemia\n\nPeaked T waves, flattened or absent P waves, progressive QRS widening toward a sine wave. Think renal failure, a missed dialysis session, crush injury, or a potassium-sparing medication.\n\n⚠️ **Do not rely on the ECG to detect this.** Physician sensitivity for identifying hyperkalemia from the tracing is poor. **Send a potassium on every wide complex tachycardia.**\n\nTreatment is calcium first, for membrane stabilization → [Hyperkalemic WCT](#wct-hyperkalemia)\n\nUsefully, sodium bicarbonate is also part of hyperkalemia management, so an empiric bicarbonate bolus does not hurt you if this turns out to be the answer.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Hypothermia\n\nBradycardia, a widened QRS, a prolonged QT, and an Osborn (J) wave — a positive deflection at the junction of the QRS and the ST segment. **Take a core temperature**, not a tympanic or oral one.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Pre-excited atrial fibrillation (Wolff-Parkinson-White with atrial fibrillation)\n\n**Irregularly irregular**, very fast (often >200 bpm), with QRS width that varies beat to beat and delta waves visible on some complexes.\n\n✅ **This is the one place in this consult where procainamide is the correct drug.** Sodium channel blockade slows conduction down the accessory pathway, which is exactly the goal.\n\n🛑 AV nodal blockers — adenosine, diltiazem, verapamil, beta-blockers, digoxin — are contraindicated. They funnel all conduction down the accessory pathway and can precipitate ventricular fibrillation.\n\n→ [WPW + AFib](#wct-wpw-afib)\n\n---\n\n**Regular versus irregular is the fastest discriminator you have.** Drug-induced sodium channel blockade produces a regular, wide, relatively slow rhythm. Pre-excited atrial fibrillation is irregular and fast.',
